questions.answer:
A class uses the class keyword with a constructor that runs on new. Methods defined in the body are shared across all instances.

code:
class User {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  greet() { return 'Hi ' + this.name; }
}
const u = new User('Ana');
console.log(u.greet());  // Hi Ana

real example:
Page Object Models model each page as a class instantiated per test with the page handle.

this refers to the instance the method is called on, unless the method is detached and called bare, which loses the binding. Arrow class fields capture this at creation.

code:
class Counter {
  n = 0;
  inc() { this.n++; }
}
const c = new Counter();
c.inc();
console.log(c.n);  // 1

real example:
Passing a page object method into an event handler requires the arrow or bind, because a bare reference loses this.

Inheritance lets a subclass reuse and specialize a base class using the extends keyword, and super calls the base constructor or methods.

code:
class BasePage {
  constructor(page) { this.page = page; }
  goto(path) { return this.page.goto(path); }
}
class LoginPage extends BasePage {
  constructor(page) { super(page); }
  async login(u, p) { await this.page.fill('#user', u); }
}

real example:
A shared BasePage with navigation and common helpers reduces duplication across page object classes.

super calls the parent constructor or a parent method. Without super(), a derived class cannot initialize and throws when instantiated.

code:
class A { constructor() { console.log('A'); } }
class B extends A { constructor() {
  super();
  console.log('B');
} }
new B();  // A then B

real example:
Subclassed page objects call super(page) first so the base locators exist before the subclass adds its own.

Static members belong to the class itself rather than instances and are called through the class name, often used for factories and constants.

code:
class Config {
  static DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;
  static create() { return new Config(); }
}
console.log(Config.DEFAULT_TIMEOUT);  // 5000

real example:
Static factory methods like User.fromApi(json) parse API responses into clean test objects.

Getters and setters expose a computed accessor, so reading and writing a property runs methods instead of touching a field directly.

code:
class Cart {
  _items = [];
  get count() { return this._items.length; }
  add(item) { this._items.push(item); }
}
const c = new Cart();
c.add('x');
console.log(c.count);  // 1

real example:
A summary getter on a page object returns the total so assertions read naturally as page.summary.

A method lives on the prototype and is shared, while a class field like count = 0 is created per instance. Fields can also be arrow functions bound to the instance.

code:
class C {
  method() {}
  field = 1;
}
const a = new C();
const b = new C();
console.log(a.field);  // 1

real example:
Per-test instance state belongs in fields so parallel tests never share mutable counters between page objects.

Use the hash prefix syntax #field, which is enforced by the language and inaccessible outside the class, unlike underscore conventions.

code:
class Wallet {
  #balance = 0;
  deposit(n) { this.#balance += n; }
}
// wallet.#balance  // SyntaxError outside the class

real example:
Private state in page objects prevents tests from tampering with internal cache fields accidentally.

Overriding means redefining a parent method in the subclass, and you can call the original with super.methodName to extend behavior.

code:
class B extends A {
  greet() {
    return super.greet() + '!extra';
  }
}

real example:
A checkout page overrides the base waitUntilLoaded to also wait for the payment iframe, then calls super for the shared part.

Classes are a cleaner syntax with strict mode, no hoisting, and you must call them with new. Constructor functions are older and more permissive.

code:
function OldUser(name) { this.name = name; }
class NewUser {}
// NewUser();  // TypeError: Class constructor cannot be invoked without 'new'

real example:
Modern test frameworks use classes for page objects, while legacy suites may still show constructor-function helpers.

Pass the page into the constructor, store it, and expose high-level methods that wrap locator actions so individual tests stay short.

code:
class LoginPage {
  constructor(page) { this.page = page; }
  async login(username, password) {
    await this.page.getByLabel('Username').fill(username);
    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' }).click();
  }
}

real example:
Each Playwright test creates the page object, calls login, and asserts on the result without touching raw selectors.
